# Package and deploy

**Prerequisites:** [Configure and secure](/docs/how-to/configure-and-secure/) and
the [ship tutorial](/docs/tutorials/ship/).
**You will do:** produce a server tree or a local macOS bundle from a working app.
**Verify:** `build --release` writes a `server` plus `assets/`.
**Next:** [Compatibility](/docs/reference/compatibility/) or
[CLI bundle](/docs/reference/cli/#bundle).

Rocci is **development** `main`. Production signing, notarization, and OS
installers are not shipped.

## Release server

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter --target x64musl
```

This packages a Linux **server** process plus `assets/`, not a macOS `.app`.
`--target` requires `--release`. Run the artifact behind your own reverse proxy;
do not expose the preview window as production.

Container sketches live under `docker/` (`rocci-ops serve app`). They consume
the release tree; they do not invent a new install channel.

## Ad-hoc macOS `.app`

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- bundle --config examples/rocci/standalone/counter/rocci.toml
```

Host-native only. `--target` is rejected for `.app`. The bundle is ad-hoc
signed. Standalone `.rocci` versus a directory with `main.roc` is the same
packaging choice as [custom main](/docs/tutorials/custom-main/): generated
dispatch versus owned routing.

**Related:** [Development and delivery](/docs/concepts/development-and-delivery/).
